  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nWe are stuck in NW flow aloft due to ridging centered in Southern \nNew Mexico and two separate low pressure systems in California \nand the mid-west. This is fueling much drier air over our area, \ncurrent ACARS sounding and water vapor analysis  very dry air \naloft between 700-400mb, but there is enough low-level moisture to\nproduce cumulus clouds across the plains and higher terrain. \nLower PWATs exist across the mountains and urban corridor while \nPWATs around 0.7-0.8 remain across the Eastern Plains. Thus model \nguidance is not excited about precip chances for the rest of today\nand this evening. Noticeably the NAMNEST came in as an outlier \nthis morning displaying the chances for showers moving through the\nurban corridor. We think the greatest chance for any isolated \ndevelopment would be near the Palmer Divide closer to midnight, \nbut chances remain very low ~10%. \n\nOn Tuesday our flow aloft shifts back to the southwest as the \naforementioned low in California drifts toward Utah bringing the \nreturn of better PWATs and moisture aloft. There should be a much\nbetter chance of afternoon/evening showers and thunderstorms \nacross the higher terrain (30-50% chance), and with 20 kts of \nmid-level flow out of the WSW, an isolated shower/storm or two \ncould make it across the plains (10-20% chance mainly west of\nUS-85). Instability should remain limited across the area so main\nthreats would be lightning and brief gusty winds to 40 mph.\n\nWednesday a weak disturbance moving in from the southwest U.S. is\nstill on track to bring better chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. Most forecast soundings indicate a fairly saturated\nupper profile, but uncertainty remains in how moist the lower \nlevels will become. The EURO indicates PWATs increase to 160-180% \nof average in the urban corridor while PWATs near 200% of average \nmay exist across the Eastern Plains. Extensive cloud cover pretty\nmuch all day long will likely inhibit development of \nshowers/storms as well, and given limited instability any\nshowers/storms that get going will not pack much of a punch\nespecially across the higher terrain and I-25 urban corridor. The\nEastern Plains may see more of a clearing in the morning hours \nincreasing the odds they see a shower/storm, and since they will \nbe closer to the better low-level moisture, there could be a few\nstronger storms that fire in the late evening hours with storm\nchances continuing past midnight. Some NWP guidance has about 1000\nJ/kg SBCAPE across the northeastern plains Wednesday evening and \nthrough midnight. Temperatures Tuesday and Wednesday should be \nnear seasonal averages, or a little warmer, with highers uppers \n70s to near 80 across the plains and urban corridor, and 60s in \nthe mountain valleys and eastern foothills.\n\nFor Thursday, a weak 500mb trough moving across Colorado \ncontinues the persistent southwest flow with PWATs remaining \nelevated through Friday evening. This will likely continue \nchances for isolated showers/storms. The greatest chances will be \nin the higher terrain and lower in the plains. Ample cloud cover \nand potential for upslope enhancement (based on the Euro ensemble \nsolutions) will mean a much cooler day with highs in the low 70s \nbut we may need to drop that further if the more ensemble output \nfavors low clouds and east upslope. In the wake of the trough on \nFriday it should be much warmer with highs returning to the low \n80s across the plains and 60s in the high country. Convective \nactivity is likely to mainly impact the mountains and Palmer \nDivide, with the best chances (40-60%) along and south of I-70 for\nnow.\n\nSaturday and Sunday we should dry out as there is high confidence\nthat ridging is expected to develop overhead once again. However, \nisolated shower/storms still cannot be ruled out as PWATs still \nremain slightly above average trapped beneath the ridge. Ensemble \nguidance indicates slight warming is expected into the upper 70s \nand low 80s across much of the area.",
